    item(11, ["Immunology", "Microbiology", "Biochemistry"], "Immune",
         "Phagocyte oxidative killing",
         "NADPH oxidase deficiency impairs the respiratory burst despite preserved neutrophil numbers and ingestion.",
         "A 4-year-old boy is evaluated after drainage of a liver abscess that grew Serratia marcescens. He previously required treatment for recurrent skin abscesses and an Aspergillus pulmonary infection. His neutrophil count and serum immunoglobulin concentrations are normal. Biopsy of a persistent lymph node shows granulomatous inflammation. Flow cytometry demonstrates markedly reduced dihydrorhodamine fluorescence after neutrophil stimulation. The cells can ingest opsonized bacteria, but intracellular killing is impaired. His mother's brother died during childhood after repeated severe bacterial infections.",
         "Which reaction is most directly impaired in this child's phagocytes?",
         [("Reduction of molecular oxygen to superoxide", "Correct. NADPH oxidase initiates the respiratory burst by transferring electrons to oxygen; its deficiency causes chronic granulomatous disease."),
          ("Conversion of hydrogen peroxide and chloride to hypochlorous acid", "Myeloperoxidase catalyzes this downstream reaction. MPO deficiency can affect DHR results, but the severe recurrent catalase-positive infections and granulomas strongly favor NADPH oxidase deficiency."),
          ("Cleavage of complement C3 to generate C3b", "C3 deficiency impairs opsonization and predisposes to bacterial infection, but does not directly abolish the stimulated intracellular oxidative burst."),
          ("Binding of leukocyte integrins to endothelial adhesion molecules", "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency causes poor tissue migration, delayed cord separation, and impaired pus formation, rather than this intracellular killing defect."),
          ("Fusion of lysosomal granules with phagosomes", "Defective granule trafficking in Chediak-Higashi syndrome is associated with giant granules and partial albinism; it is not the oxidase reaction tested here.")],
         "Recurrent Serratia and Aspergillus infections, granulomas, and impaired stimulated DHR oxidation support chronic granulomatous disease. NADPH oxidase fails to generate adequate superoxide and downstream reactive oxygen species. Phagocytosis and circulating neutrophil numbers can remain normal; the defect is in oxidative killing.",
         "Distinguish phagocyte ingestion, migration, and oxidative killing defects.",
         "Catalase-positive infections + granulomas + abnormal oxidative-burst assay -> chronic granulomatous disease -> impaired superoxide generation.",
         [ref("robbins", "Chapter 6: Diseases of the Immune System", "250")]),

